ctlsock: an open loop cannot LEARN a gain — seed it (MAME_CTL_GAIN_X/Y)

Applies AFTER mame-ctlsock-move-step-cap.patch (same file, later hunks).

THE BUG. MOVEA has two modes. Closed loop (MAME_CTL_CURSOR_ITEMS set) reads
the guest's cursor registers every window and re-derives the error from the
truth, so a wrong counts->px gain costs convergence SPEED and nothing else.
Open loop — every machine with no readable cursor register, which is every
MAME tile outside the SGI — has no reading at all: movea_target()'s degraded
branch states each target as a DELTA and hands it straight to the paced queue

	move_paced_travel(x - px, y - py);          // PIXELS, issued as COUNTS

while the belief that computed `px` moves in px through the gain
(move_rel(): `m_bx += dx * m_gx`). The two only agree when the gain is 1.0,
and 1.0 is exactly what the module has: m_gx/m_gy are LEARNED, learn_gain()
is called only from observe(), and observe() runs only in the closed loop. So
in open loop the gain is 1.0 forever and MOVEA issues Delta-px counts at a
guest whose ratio is not 1.

Measured on apple2e (Apple //e enhanced, Apple II Mouse Card in slot 4,
Dazzle Draw canvas, MAME_CTL_PTR_MOD=256): 1.547 guest px per count on X,
1.674 on Y. A 1.0 gain therefore issues ~65% of the counts a target needs and
the belief believes it arrived — MOVEA under-shoots ~35-40% and every
subsequent relative target inherits the error, because the belief is the only
origin an open loop has. MAME_CTL_CAL_X/Y does not help: it calibrates the
CURSOR REGISTER reading and is inert without one.

THE FIX, three parts.

  MAME_CTL_GAIN_X / MAME_CTL_GAIN_Y   guest px per mouse count, float,
  default 1.0 (today's behaviour). Seeds m_gx/m_gy at setup, and a state load
  reseeds to the SEED rather than to 1.0 — the reseed comment's reasoning
  ("the learned gain describes the guest screen the session was ON") is about
  a LEARNED value; a measured constant of the machine survives a restore.

  A px->counts converter for the open-loop branch (px_to_counts /
  move_paced_travel_px): counts = round(Delta-px / gain), with a one-count
  floor so a sub-gain residue still moves. The belief then integrates
  counts * gain, both sides live in px, and a restated target re-derives
  whatever the rounding left.

  The queued-remainder clamp simulation in the same branch converted nothing:
  it added queue entries' COUNTS to a PX belief. Same gain, applied.

Every default is 1.0, so a station that does not set the knobs builds and
behaves exactly as before.

MAME_CTL_SCREEN already exists (WxH clamp surface, default 1288x1024) and is
what a station must set to its published MAME_SHM_SIZE geometry; apple2e's
fixture now does. No new knob for that.

MEASURED after this patch, apple2e/Dazzle Draw, GAIN_X=1.547 GAIN_Y=1.674,
SCREEN=1024x768, arrow tip located in the framebuffer: the SCALE is now right --
two INTERIOR targets 600 px apart (no clamp between them) land at 0.988x of
commanded on X and 0.940x on Y, refining the seeds to 1.529/1.573. The ORIGIN
is still wrong, and this patch does not fix it: the same degraded branch homes
with a hardcoded `18 * m_move_step` COUNT slam, and after it the first target
lands ~147 px short on X and ~85 px short on Y -- the guest owes back part of
the overshoot before the arrow moves again -- and an open loop's only origin is
its own belief, so the deficit never clears. Five targets (300,200) (700,500)
(150,600) (900,100) (300,200) landed -289,-180 and -148,-88 where the arrow was
locatable at all. NEXT: size that slam in PIXELS through the gain, not in raw
counts, and re-measure.

